#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6179bfd3-ad1a-57d5-9e85-07e8de87a9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3}}{x y z}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3}}{x y z}\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\frac{x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2}}{x y z}\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524158} {:answer/id #uuid "6544e835-303b-5d55-9ff4-bf9b2ff7f858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(- \\frac{x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}}{x^{2} y z}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}}{x y^{2} z}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\frac{x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}}{x y z^{2}}\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524159} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4a1fb2-b6f5-56ce-8ca2-8933d289d531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2 x y)\\,i+(y^{2})\\,j+(- 2 z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524163} {:answer/id #uuid "e45afc2c-89cc-5d31-a291-04df0e43e302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y^{2})\\,i+(2 x y)\\,j+(- 2 z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524190} {:answer/id #uuid "fe10362b-4bcf-503f-8c49-8dc2e3a0aca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3}}{x y z} + \\frac{x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}}{x^{2} y z})\\,i+(\\frac{3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3}}{x y z} + \\frac{x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}}{x y^{2} z})\\,j+(\\frac{x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2}}{x y z} + \\frac{x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}}{x y z^{2}})\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524190} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bf2288-62d0-5f23-9d2d-c0fb16e9c57f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af2e4ace-5861-5b18-b355-92218c8fda1e" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)g(x,y,z),$ given that $f(x,y,z)=x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3}$ and $g(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x y z}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y,z)$ and $g(x,y,z)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})=2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})=3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})=x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}k=(2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3})\\,i+(3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3})\\,j+(x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $g(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)=- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}i+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}j+\\frac{∂g}{∂z}k=\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y,z)g(x,y,z)] & =g(x,y,z)∇f(x,y,z)+f(x,y,z)∇g(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)((2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3})\\,i+(3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3})\\,j+(x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2})\\,k)+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})(\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(2 x y^{3} z - y z^{3})i+\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(3 x^{2} y^{2} z - x z^{3})j+\\left(\\frac{1}{x y z}\\right)(x^{2} y^{3} - 3 x y z^{2})k+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\\right)i+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\\right)j+(x^{2} y^{3} z - x y z^{3})\\left(- \\frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\\right)k \\\\  & =(2 y^{2} - \\frac{z^{2}}{x})i+(3 x y - \\frac{z^{2}}{y})j+(\\frac{x y^{2}}{z} - 3 z)k+(- y^{2} + \\frac{z^{2}}{x})i+(- x y + \\frac{z^{2}}{y})j+(- \\frac{x y^{2}}{z} + z)k \\\\  & =(y^{2})\\,i+(2 x y)\\,j+(- 2 z)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b12bed-f89f-57b2-8ae2-8204dcf0665f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 4 y)\\,i+(4 x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524146} {:answer/id #uuid "2b97234c-25d6-5347-947c-3537be088001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4 y)\\,i+(- 4 x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524148} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1327c2-cfad-55ec-8554-d21c72bd153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2 x + 2 y)\\,i+(- 2 x - 2 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524170} {:answer/id #uuid "92425297-e09b-57c4-89af-eed5f27f56b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4 x)\\,i+(- 4 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524172} {:answer/id #uuid "f779ad7d-fb31-50f4-9978-b6dc57b1e6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2 x - 2 y)\\,i+(2 x - 2 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524172} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ab0f1ac-375d-502a-af53-a56056488308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4b60a1d-d5a3-5373-aeeb-9fd900ce0e06" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =- 2 x + 2 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(-2)\\,i+(2)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =- x - y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(-1)\\,i+(-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(- x - y)((-2)\\,i+(2)\\,j)+(- 2 x + 2 y)((-1)\\,i+(-1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- x - y)(-2)i+(- x - y)(2)j+(- 2 x + 2 y)(-1)i+(- 2 x + 2 y)(-1)j \\\\  & =(2 x + 2 y)i+(- 2 x - 2 y)j+(2 x - 2 y)i+(2 x - 2 y)j \\\\  & =(4 x)\\,i+(- 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c98ea29-bdf4-535c-8905-0e665688e1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 3 x + 3 y)\\,i+(4 x - 4 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524141} {:answer/id #uuid "716903f3-1915-51b9-b21c-19085f2bd116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 6 x + 7 y)\\,i+(7 x - 8 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524164} {:answer/id #uuid "79fb8e43-61f5-507f-bcf8-885672627bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y)\\,i+(- x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524166} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae708ac-d48e-5c03-8083-d7951c5f7722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 3 x + 4 y)\\,i+(3 x - 4 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524173} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf65bb7-ba35-584e-a9ca-b15343ee8ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(7 x - 8 y)\\,i+(- 6 x + 7 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524164} :answer-field/id #uuid "135677e6-fc24-52c5-a111-90477ff87ed5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c004028d-833d-577a-87c2-e3fd0d71dbfc" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =- x + y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(-1)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =3 x - 4 y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(3 x - 4 y)((-1)\\,i+(1)\\,j)+(- x + y)((3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3 x - 4 y)(-1)i+(3 x - 4 y)(1)j+(- x + y)(3)i+(- x + y)(-4)j \\\\  & =(- 3 x + 4 y)i+(3 x - 4 y)j+(- 3 x + 3 y)i+(4 x - 4 y)j \\\\  & =(- 6 x + 7 y)\\,i+(7 x - 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a9a90e-3590-57ce-857e-4738e4b8a96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 18 y)\\,i+(18 x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524140} {:answer/id #uuid "17f10e04-4c44-5e8c-9a56-7bc02d440d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 12 x - 12 y)\\,i+(6 x + 6 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524143} {:answer/id #uuid "65d4613a-986c-595a-b8e7-1e27d6799631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 12 x + 6 y)\\,i+(- 12 x + 6 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524162} {:answer/id #uuid "898bd464-6f82-5875-8fac-53de974047ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 24 x - 6 y)\\,i+(- 6 x + 12 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524169} {:answer/id #uuid "b64fca02-3c60-59b5-9c60-0a6db140aa04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 6 x + 12 y)\\,i+(- 24 x - 6 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524169} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc44bad-0972-526a-a5f1-5498b898990c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2782dea-5fc4-5e05-abad-5b0c5268e377" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =4 x - 2 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =- 3 x - 3 y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(-3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - 3 y)((4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j)+(4 x - 2 y)((-3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - 3 y)(4)i+(- 3 x - 3 y)(-2)j+(4 x - 2 y)(-3)i+(4 x - 2 y)(-3)j \\\\  & =(- 12 x - 12 y)i+(6 x + 6 y)j+(- 12 x + 6 y)i+(- 12 x + 6 y)j \\\\  & =(- 24 x - 6 y)\\,i+(- 6 x + 12 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b3961c-518e-5fce-accc-1c06b8b99b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4} + \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4})\\,i+(\\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4} - \\left(x + 1\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524150} {:answer/id #uuid "65a001d5-1a9b-591e-90e3-44eedf041dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4})\\,i+(\\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524161} {:answer/id #uuid "884570ab-4fe3-58b8-bdc0-6fceffcbf5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 5\\right) e^{x + y + 5})\\,i+(\\left(x + 2\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + y + 5})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524168} {:answer/id #uuid "913eb3b4-3e71-5d25-b83a-4d1c81d9ed5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4})\\,i+(\\left(x + 1\\right) e^{x + 1} e^{y + 4})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524171} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f21048-bf93-5345-9339-7789610f673c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(x + 2\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + y + 5})\\,i+(\\left(x + 1\\right) \\left(y + 5\\right) e^{x + y + 5})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524191}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524191} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d71dcf6-8f66-56fb-9f80-76a6a0f2f0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ed54f79d-76c2-5f18-8276-7aa2bb85a756" :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}$ and $h(x,y)=(y+4){e}^{(x+1)}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+1){e}^{(y+4)})={e}^{(y+4)} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+1){e}^{(y+4)})=(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y+4){e}^{(x+1)})=(y+4){e}^{(x+1)} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+4){e}^{(x+1)})={e}^{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=(y+4){e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}((y+4){e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x+1)}\\text{ }j)+(y+4){e}^{(x+1)}({e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(x+1)(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j+(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1)(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =((x+1)+1)(y+4){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x+1)(1+(y+4)){e}^{(x+1)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c760db6b-0eeb-5554-9025-e5a99fbe051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524308} {:answer/id #uuid "d7df9a75-b991-51ad-a2a4-a026aa7a6826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524315} {:answer/id #uuid "e278da23-c9a5-5553-ba6d-afc002d9451f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524317} {:answer/id #uuid "e6643984-2a22-59da-8678-6d7ee1f2be62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524319} {:answer/id #uuid "eb183123-0734-58ce-bdf6-9cb78f3679d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524315} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf7078c-3066-5b36-a14c-26d5fe9f4b4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "18deea5a-b54f-52df-a2ec-2c79bc3b4021" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,2)$, $B(3,4,2)$, $C(5,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+2k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(5i+4j+2k)-(2i+j+2k) \\\\  & =3i+3j \\\\  & =⟨3,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-6k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ 6 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524280 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58eb9c5b-dc36-5c5a-9c5d-5db3dd5fb2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524290} :answer-field/id #uuid "0daf3e2b-7bd4-528e-ab6e-ac7f1722d756" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c269e70-e4c2-5782-b1f2-15eb40534125" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,-1,0)$, $B(-3,2,0)$, $C(-2,2,0).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+2j)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i+2j)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229676e2-dcee-59b9-ac83-b20a4270b240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524277} {:answer/id #uuid "4c08211f-c680-5026-8003-236d00832455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524284} {:answer/id #uuid "76ad2a10-0b27-593c-866f-1b653450933c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524294} {:answer/id #uuid "aa58d458-8bc4-59fb-8f35-0f398d63757a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524300} {:answer/id #uuid "efbaee34-0ddf-53c6-a655-0bba9f675d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524300} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5dcdb30-dc6e-5eee-974d-dcf4125881e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f822573-7fea-5604-880b-62800c75dfc1" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,0,0)$, $B(-3,1,0)$, $C(1,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+j)-(-i) \\\\  & =-2i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+2j)-(-i) \\\\  & =2i+2j \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-6k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 6 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b92164-9dcd-525a-a37a-83d4dd33a13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524269} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec8d433-d4ad-5c6a-af58-d6d95e54203b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524278} {:answer/id #uuid "50f5fffe-6a62-5dac-8974-c17a5ba7913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524286} {:answer/id #uuid "7aec265c-e512-5201-b64d-7ac556d41c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524296} {:answer/id #uuid "cd17ffd7-496c-5185-8571-3ec9d6f65456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524269} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0bcdee-48c0-51cd-9239-5ca0f8cdfb94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d5e0ac3-99c8-577c-8173-57e8823ce5b8" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,1,1)$, $B(-1,4,1)$, $C(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i+4j+k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ 1 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1091ff47-f3af-50cb-a41f-b8b277adce73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524273} {:answer/id #uuid "16b34727-1d4d-502d-963b-fbcd77f6c315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{205}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524274} {:answer/id #uuid "58b3f040-734c-504b-8198-828143494618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524289} {:answer/id #uuid "b140a14c-6832-57f4-9368-6af81b1a1257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{205}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524303} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9dd6ce-4ccf-53e9-8263-0f085e6b1f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{205}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524322} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ce5d229-638a-5975-b0d8-6ba86d3bfa72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a0d3f715-ae56-57fd-a913-2f3201809016" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(0,-2,-1)$, $B(1,0,-1)$, $C(2,-4,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i-k)-(-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+2j \\\\  & =⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i-4j-2k)-(-2j-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-2i+j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{41} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{205}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b273cc-e0cb-5b4a-bf8e-08e164ef574f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524279} {:answer/id #uuid "ab936a9b-afc4-5992-b435-f50791f43bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1469}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524301} {:answer/id #uuid "b98ac02b-3680-56d5-a6c0-b6858e537ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{113}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524305} {:answer/id #uuid "c78247b2-069e-5217-8274-18ba7cd876bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{1469}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524310} {:answer/id #uuid "e55d19b7-1d9f-5091-8cf2-fb59199b5bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1469}}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524301} :answer-field/id #uuid "5488e764-1624-5ce9-8fc4-119640f324ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bab7c594-ddc6-56dd-ac0c-e9c3f81e8f61" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(0,0,2)$, $B(2,-3,2)$, $C(2,2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-3j+2k)-2k \\\\  & =2i-3j \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j+3k)-2k \\\\  & =2i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =-3i-2j+10k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{113} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{113}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{113} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1469}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d9e9b3-2d93-5c7c-a240-38d2ff154a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1390}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524288} :answer-field/id #uuid "1727fa5e-2a2e-5770-9ba1-478762cba716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5fb10aa-6d76-5175-b7b5-63e97ab5d5b0" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,-2,-1)$, $B(2,1,-1)$, $C(4,0,2).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+2k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =9i-3j-7k \\\\  & =⟨9,-3,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{139} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{139}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{139} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{139}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1390}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea8d1d5-d780-55e7-8121-829b6b055bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{890}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524272} {:answer/id #uuid "4992b11f-dcb0-572b-b8e7-3614405c5615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524283} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8757b3-e271-5dd1-8931-b8ae262df176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{89}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524297} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c60a5f-4c2f-53aa-b4d2-8859f558b1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{890}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524302} {:answer/id #uuid "d08d0f9a-6d84-5472-8d73-d24f2891ff3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{890}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524302} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe11181-fdbd-5b0e-8182-1027855c2a3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c777f95f-b65a-5504-8634-9b81d6d6469b" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,-2,1)$, $B(2,1,1)$, $C(4,0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-(i-2j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i-k)-(i-2j+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-6i+2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-6,2,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{89} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{89}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{89} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{89}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{890}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d5ce066-5d51-5c77-912e-e3b8d17b68fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{182}}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524292} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c799e02-e8cf-5ca8-9912-9cd264282be3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cddadf64-7b0d-5387-8a0d-b37744150f43" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(-1,-1,0)$, $B(-3,2,0)$, $C(-2,2,3).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+2j)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i+2j+3k)-(-i-j) \\\\  & =-i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =9i+6j-3k \\\\  & =⟨9,6,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{126} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{14} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{182}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "490ee772-b2a4-58b2-b9b8-0563b999f584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524282} :answer-field/id #uuid "d782b15c-9fda-5505-bb66-7a3bb9168c0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dbaf3eeb-bcf5-58dc-be46-6ca8a559b6d2" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(1,-2,-1)$, $B(2,1,-1)$, $C(4,0,-1).$\n\n$CH=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+j-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =i+3j \\\\  & =⟨1,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i-k)-(i-2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 0)i-(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-7k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ 7 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{10}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0e0626-6248-5b0f-8302-6f527732a13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526559} {:answer/id #uuid "253ca439-cd38-5c0a-a233-00c9ea58b381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526584} {:answer/id #uuid "48db2746-13e6-5da9-a526-f85be7161a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526647} {:answer/id #uuid "6545d507-c4ab-5302-a22d-62c4ef0a3e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526705} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd0a5cd-65c6-58a5-ac60-ab333212dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526584} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf514b2-8d3a-5a58-a6b9-5e62c2d37fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" :question/math-academy-id "q-130720" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{3}{y}^{2}=2x+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{y}^{2} & =2x+y \\\\ {x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2)\\,i+(2{x}^{3}y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =(3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(2(-1)^{3}(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,1)=⟨1,-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x+1) & =y-1 \\\\ -3x-3 & =y-1 \\\\ 3x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc28fd4-0332-5dca-9124-fbc2512f4f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526573} {:answer/id #uuid "47b2da7a-a030-51c5-b002-05391f108200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526643} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f30ca4-75c7-5d71-83ef-390f43fece84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526890} {:answer/id #uuid "e2579daa-2b83-50d8-a9be-1387a386e9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526945} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e5fc00-b4cc-5045-8cb1-ac186e6219f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526643} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14cf0d4-83ef-5fb3-b242-66382b80f326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bf356ec-77aa-55f5-9268-fd51c57a8d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71400" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j+\\frac{1}{1}\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e50d33-12b9-5c12-ac4e-4e3135ef41df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527394} {:answer/id #uuid "8b05db23-972c-567b-8223-11bddcab9b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527583} {:answer/id #uuid "92ad03ae-93a6-5758-8869-8e58634200a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527597} {:answer/id #uuid "a18fa886-a66f-5c1c-a378-38efae13d666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527639} {:answer/id #uuid "f113768c-cf48-5fce-945b-53b72f33cbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "39{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527777} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3bc565-6668-5708-9572-740391604ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f9d1bf2-2ca6-5610-aed1-5f5054aae288" :question/math-academy-id "q-124272" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5f5b235b54d2acd20297ab365d9b93907111b3fec9a86776c28e93fdfe105ee.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,3t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t)^{2}+(3t)^{2} \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{2} \\\\  & =13{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}13{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =39{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21702398-6680-552d-923f-027dff95a35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527383} {:answer/id #uuid "6029d8a7-a6ae-5959-9ee4-f8e3ebd8e072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527489} {:answer/id #uuid "78ae96ca-4942-5362-807b-c0b65d2496c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527533} {:answer/id #uuid "c94fc212-35ff-5e38-9f57-12d4f4959a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527711} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8f272e-677d-5489-96d8-1e29db88e990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527489} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9c47c5-4e96-58fa-ac91-5791dc11f30c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ada3d128-ac86-51c2-88c8-3e672e4cad88" :question/math-academy-id "q-124172" :question/problem "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-2x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+2t)^{2}-2(2t) \\\\  & =1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ba6004-1436-5e5b-ae2f-735e7eb45f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-3)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527311} {:answer/id #uuid "1b787a8e-ad68-5398-92d2-2ebf1e8be534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527366} {:answer/id #uuid "5550e6c3-7324-5809-9dfc-0f8be5c60c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527469} {:answer/id #uuid "78816eec-e214-5a4a-a51d-fa5102203d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527532} {:answer/id #uuid "9756d62a-6b6c-5317-a183-008b9c4d2660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527469} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86ee8d9-aeae-5ab1-b4a0-98c765570dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca5fbd9d-06c2-5516-a2aa-caaa1b2a1fc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124246" :question/problem "![](images/88/88da0f1ddf1f605186c9d921a9eade24b5f0aa9de2ae4afdc51a921586093b16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,3)$ to the point $(0,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,3)$ and $(0,0).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(0-(-2))),\\,(3+t(0-3))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,3-3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2t-2)^{2}(3-3t) \\\\  & =4(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3(1-t) \\\\  & =-12(t-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-12(t-1)^{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-24{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c605152-901c-5bcd-a252-1ab9cc3b85cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v - 5=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528089} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccdd355-67c6-5566-941a-b4d92d487572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w - 5=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528091} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6eefd5-a305-5c5a-a520-b6206a7fba35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u + 5=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528103} {:answer/id #uuid "849ed888-6be5-543b-abea-d73096054d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u - 5=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528107} {:answer/id #uuid "de8bd9d8-b442-57a1-9562-0a7b92d7a125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528107} :answer-field/id #uuid "b12d9169-ece2-5f8e-8851-d1d9eb933e79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13f0dd3d-1f22-5629-9241-573d35d65b91" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(u - 5\\right)^{2} \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\left(u - 5\\right)^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 u - 10 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 u - 10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u - 10)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u - 10)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(u - 5\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(u - 5\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $u - 5=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c5ea31-efda-54a4-824d-91f820e8d0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528083} {:answer/id #uuid "838578b3-3790-5776-a4b4-78337049deeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w + 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528106} {:answer/id #uuid "950067b2-bb47-5fdd-8170-76f27c144f53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v + 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528111} {:answer/id #uuid "e5804f4d-5f9e-5bb8-8479-505629b0e11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528133} {:answer/id #uuid "e72cbe6b-50cf-5c52-be21-06b2588b4072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u + 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528111} :answer-field/id #uuid "092be8c7-b2b3-55de-a763-e9f8866ddd74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "508d0b30-7080-5a0a-bcfa-a8afc4979721" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ \\left(v + 3\\right)^{2} \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=\\left(v + 3\\right)^{2},\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2 v + 6 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 v + 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 v + 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(2 v + 6\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(v + 3\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(v + 3\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $v + 3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0544780e-14fc-55aa-87a9-4cf97780c7b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u - 4=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528073} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac687cb-668d-5381-8fcb-bc9f45907122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528076} {:answer/id #uuid "243e7eac-20cf-57d0-9916-6cd6a9affdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u + 4=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528082} {:answer/id #uuid "6188d987-5745-5f88-ab24-c44d274474c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v + 4=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528097} {:answer/id #uuid "be7ae697-cd3e-5479-8ce6-7405cb76bcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w + 4=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528123}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528082} :answer-field/id #uuid "73995b19-08e6-5555-850a-91dc308e452a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5623f019-5b29-5f61-95d8-7be3055bcc58" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(u + 4\\right)^{2} \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\left(u + 4\\right)^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 u + 8 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 8)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 8)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(u + 4\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(u + 4\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $u + 4=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e0e693a-bf37-56cc-8164-00f372dad8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528085} {:answer/id #uuid "47c6ada3-a010-56e0-b46b-ae779b5a5a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528087} {:answer/id #uuid "b88e5b00-228f-52e3-8b9f-d0daf74eff3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w + 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528121} {:answer/id #uuid "c0886689-a8e4-56c9-b290-4b350e321665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528124} {:answer/id #uuid "c380da40-2559-5607-aa89-97268521c7a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528085} :answer-field/id #uuid "a216c380-f1cd-5afe-ae7d-6797caccf711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "828da370-4d6a-5438-a9e4-3248998e835e" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ \\left(w - 3\\right)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=\\left(w - 3\\right)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =2 w - 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 w - 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 w - 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(2 w - 6)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(w - 3\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(w - 3\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $w - 3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e0ebbd-8383-5c1b-a7c9-4716ac9c4cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $16 u + 5=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528075} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae41af4-df52-5568-92f7-9afd52f5550d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $10 u + 4=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528077} {:answer/id #uuid "29353d26-c816-529b-a53d-9616a3cd2090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $10 u + 5=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528084} {:answer/id #uuid "ae558e9c-6c07-5dc0-80dd-f187e7aa012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $22 u + 5=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528117} {:answer/id #uuid "b236eac3-d86c-5cb2-8a40-76e176e96493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $1 - 6 u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528084} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5b9202d-16e9-5fe8-8a86-ac05c266ac48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9192e177-97db-5aba-a9ef-4ed8595bf5b0" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u - 3 v - 2 w \\\\ - u^{2} + v \\\\ 4 u^{2} + 2 u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u - 3 v - 2 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=- u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=4 u^{2} + 2 u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-3 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =- 2 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =8 u + 2 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ - 2 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 u + 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(3)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 0 \\\\ 8 u + 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 1 \\\\ 8 u + 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(3)(- 2 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(8 u + 2))+(-2)(- 2 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(8 u + 2)) \\\\  & =5 \\left(2 u + 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 5 \\left(2 u + 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $10 u + 5=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb84bc4-7b45-548e-a2fa-9858922eca5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $6 u - 2=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528081} {:answer/id #uuid "4d84d0f3-3275-5916-8633-e0db4b4fb7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $6 u - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528092} {:answer/id #uuid "72856a29-c86d-552c-a63a-1b5dc73976b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $- 10 u - 2=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528100} {:answer/id #uuid "a617f30b-32a8-54ec-b573-dc1cd8d1f259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $8 u + 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528115} {:answer/id #uuid "f842f30b-6f61-5ed3-882a-e4618de3d746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $- 2 u - 2=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528081} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1bef16-c94d-59b0-9fff-4586e97270e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "93ef6ca3-680b-56f1-b387-2cba8288a0cb" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + 4 v - w \\\\ - u^{2} + v \\\\ - u^{2} - 3 u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u + 4 v - w,\\,y(u,v,w)=- u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=- u^{2} - 3 u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =- 2 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =- 2 u - 3 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ - 2 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ - 2 u - 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 0 \\\\ - 2 u - 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}- 2 u & 1 \\\\ - 2 u - 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(-4)(- 2 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(- 2 u - 3))+(-1)(- 2 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(- 2 u - 3)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(3 u - 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(3 u - 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $6 u - 2=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8103d5-1d1d-5539-8a25-78db0f5145f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $- 28 u - 2=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528078} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb0e10d-b288-5c1d-aac9-e69ffceddfec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $4 u - 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528096} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e85cfe-b159-56b8-b8f5-f5869b13e490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $- 28 u - 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528116} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a3c49c-fbe3-52d1-a80e-087d00635c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $36 u - 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528120} {:answer/id #uuid "f9524462-e914-593a-bd38-f2a07ae601da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $1 - 32 u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528116} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e0a85e-166a-5bfd-8b30-c8fcd72abd92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cb745fb6-ba80-5128-902d-8b91b00fe021" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u - 4 v - 2 w \\\\ - 4 u^{2} + v \\\\ u^{2} - u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u - 4 v - 2 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=- 4 u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=u^{2} - u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-4 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =- 8 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 u - 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & -2 \\\\ - 8 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 u - 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(4)\\begin{vmatrix}- 8 u & 0 \\\\ 2 u - 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 8 u & 1 \\\\ 2 u - 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(4)(- 8 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(2 u - 1))+(-2)(- 8 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(2 u - 1)) \\\\  & =- 28 u - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ - 28 u - 1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $- 28 u - 1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb95208-de1d-5107-937c-4b95392cabfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $4 u + 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528095} {:answer/id #uuid "8b151ea6-6bf3-50da-b2cf-48829c626b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $20 u - 2=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528108} {:answer/id #uuid "956a3402-0229-59fc-81ea-108ffa280642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $28 u - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528112} {:answer/id #uuid "f94ff84b-c560-53eb-b9d7-b92cca8a9bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $24 u - 2=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528139} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5273c3-d427-52c4-92ab-a1a09822e996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $28 u - 2=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528141}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528141} :answer-field/id #uuid "179dd747-8255-5b2c-99ee-546770d66eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dcb5cc43-b555-53b7-8a05-e6eed19a5fa4" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u - 2 v + 3 w \\\\ u^{2} + v \\\\ - 4 u^{2} + u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u - 2 v + 3 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=- 4 u^{2} + u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 - 8 u & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 - 8 u & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 u & 0 \\\\ 1 - 8 u & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(3)\\begin{vmatrix}2 u & 1 \\\\ 1 - 8 u & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(2)(2 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(1 - 8 u))+(3)(2 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(1 - 8 u)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(14 u - 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(14 u - 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $28 u - 2=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45da639a-a9c4-5df9-b985-bd60ab49e03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $22 u - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528086} {:answer/id #uuid "4cccb54a-2323-54cc-8771-d66a1ea28d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $10 u - 4=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528090} {:answer/id #uuid "a312df95-1798-5422-bdd8-51cc96fbf230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $16 u - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528114} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cddbd1-7645-57a2-987f-d4a400510d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $1 - 6 u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528127} {:answer/id #uuid "f11adf19-097e-5e21-9369-b018ddc048dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $10 u - 3=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe2c2ef2-86c0-5fe7-bba6-8d0d09672c0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dee05f83-d6b9-5885-962f-b5d399b7ef4b" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + v - 4 w \\\\ 3 u^{2} + v \\\\ 2 u^{2} - u + w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u + v - 4 w,\\,y(u,v,w)=3 u^{2} + v,\\,z(u,v,w)=2 u^{2} - u + w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =6 u & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =4 u - 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 6 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 u - 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}6 u & 0 \\\\ 4 u - 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\begin{vmatrix}6 u & 1 \\\\ 4 u - 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0))+(-1)(6 u\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(4 u - 1))+(-4)(6 u\\cdot(0)-(1)\\cdot(4 u - 1)) \\\\  & =10 u - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 10 u - 3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $10 u - 3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6843c54c-f17b-5834-b153-e5b5a1efb011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u + 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528098} {:answer/id #uuid "74f168eb-ccb1-5380-b363-e871472ffa30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v + 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528102} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a304f5-81ed-5e87-be99-54f35f1ec0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u - 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528122} {:answer/id #uuid "de769aee-8b75-51ef-a2c0-08755022682c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w + 1=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528129} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b4dd00-64f4-5b00-8e49-b71031fa0857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528098} :answer-field/id #uuid "813dd0bb-8482-5415-8c3e-9e6f5842ffd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dfb5d07b-82a7-5cc7-b48f-5f51fbec3e21" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(u + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\left(u + 1\\right)^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 u + 2 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 u + 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 u + 2)(1\\cdot(1)-(0)\\cdot(0)) \\\\  & =2 \\left(u + 1\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2 \\left(u + 1\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical points lie on the plane $u + 1=0.$"}]}
